      "page_idx": 10,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.065,
            0.379,
            0.082
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 6"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.12,
            0.886,
            0.233
          ],
          "content": "In the above example, we saw that + 2000 – (– 200) = + 2000 + (+ 200) = + 2200. In other words, subtracting a negative number is the same as adding the corresponding positive number. That is, we can replace subtraction of a negative number by addition of a positive number!"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.239,
            0.787,
            0.309
          ],
          "content": "In the other exercises that you did above, did you notice that subtracting a negative number was the same as adding the corresponding positive number?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.318,
            0.787,
            0.364
          ],
          "content": "Take a look at the ‘infinite lift’ above. Does it remind you of a number line? In what ways?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.805,
            0.239,
            0.925,
            0.354
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_10_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.376,
            0.463,
            0.397
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Back to the number line"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.404,
            0.886,
            0.519
          ],
          "content": "The ‘infinite lift’ we saw above looked very much like a number line, didn’t it? In fact, if we rotate it by 90°, it basically becomes a number line. It also tells us how to complete the number ray to a number line, answering the question that we had asked at the beginning of the chapter. To the left of 0 are the negative numbers – 1, – 2, – 3, …"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.526,
            0.885,
            0.572
          ],
          "content": "Usually we drop the ‘+’ sign on positive numbers and simply write them as 1, 2, 3, …"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.583,
            0.89,
            0.613
          ],
          "content": "|  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| -10 | -9 | 8 |  |  |  |  | 4 | 3 |  |  |  | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 7 | 8 | 9 | 10 |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_10_table_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.625,
            0.884,
            0.694
          ],
          "content": "Instead of traveling along the number line using a lift, we can simply imagine walking on it. To the right is the positive (forward) direction, and to the left is the negative (backward) direction."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.701,
            0.885,
            0.769
          ],
          "content": "Smaller numbers are now to the left of bigger numbers and bigger numbers are to the right of smaller numbers. So, 2 < 5; –3 < 2; and –5 < –3."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.794,
            0.885,
            0.839
          ],
          "content": "If, from 5 you wish to go over to 9, how far must you travel along the number line?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.16,
            0.841,
            0.887,
            0.899
          ],
